+\subsection{Reader Roadmap and Review Contract}
+The paper is organized as a straight pipeline rather than a leaderboard narrative. First, it states the claim boundary. Second, it explains the synthetic scenario and signal generation. Third, it defines the detector-view contract that strips labels, split keys, group identifiers, time locks, and generator internals before modeling. Fourth, it evaluates accepted radar/cue branches and accepted human-engineered fusion. Fifth, it introduces EI as a train/CV-only search and sparse calibrated late-fusion layer. Finally, it reports one blind-holdout score after the selection lock and pushes code, model cards, and command lines into the appendix.
+
+\begin{claimbox}
+\reviewrail{Review contract.} A reviewer should be able to answer four questions without reading generator internals: (i) what public-proxy object and hard-negative families were sampled, (ii) what radar/cue products were generated, (iii) what detector-facing features were allowed, and (iv) what evidence proves that selection, calibration, and thresholding used train/CV rows only. The answer is visible in the data-processing trace, detector-view schema, selection lock, EI evolution trace, and source-code appendix.
+\end{claimbox}
