+\subsection{Simulation Best Practices and Monte Carlo Design}
+A synthetic radar benchmark is only useful when it is explicit about what is sampled, what is held fixed, and what is hidden from the detector. EchoForge therefore follows a scenario-group Monte Carlo design: a group samples public-proxy object family, site archetype, range band, aspect, weather/noise regime, hard-negative role, and seed-derived nuisance variables; the group then expands into three phase records, initial take-up, climb transition, and cruise altitude. The split is assigned before phase expansion so that all records from one physical group remain on the same side of the train/CV--holdout boundary.
+
+The scenario model is intentionally conservative. Positive groups use a fixed-wing pusher-prop public proxy with broad geometry, kinematic, RCS, and micro-Doppler envelopes. Hard negatives include single birds, bird flocks, RC fixed-wing aircraft, weather cells, ground vehicles, wind turbines, multipath ghosts, RFI bursts, terrain glint, and clutter-only counterfactuals. Noise and impairment sampling covers thermal/SNR stress, Weibull/K-like clutter, sea and weather clutter, vegetation motion, AGC compression, clock drift, quantization, dropped CPI, Doppler folding, calibration offset, and passive-RF missingness.
+
+Best practice in this benchmark is not to make the synthetic scene look clean; it is to make false alarms plausible, audit the split, and publish the assumptions. The positive holdout is deliberately reported as small rather than hidden: 24 positive records from eight positive scenario groups. That is why the primary KPI is a group-block lower confidence bound, and why phase and family slices are diagnostic rather than treated as stable field-rate estimates.
 
