+\subsection{Signal-Processing Contract}
+The signal-processing contract has four stages. The first stage generates target and clutter terms in complex IQ space. The second stage forms detector-visible summaries such as range-Doppler concentration, Doppler spread, micro-Doppler cadence bands, clutter stress, track confidence, source quality, and cue consistency. The third stage denies labels, split roles, group IDs, time locks, and generator-only metadata. The fourth stage emits train/CV and holdout detector-view CSVs with the same schema. This lets the detector see realistic stress summaries while preventing shortcut learning from audit fields.
+
+\section{Detector Processing Best Practices}
+EchoForge reports classical radar branches, learned detector controls, accepted fusion, and EI on the same detector-view contract. The classical path is intentionally familiar: windowed CPI products, range-Doppler concentration, CFAR-style thresholding, MTD-style Doppler concentration, track confirmation, and source-quality penalties. CFAR and detection-theory references define the baseline framing, while micro-Doppler references bound the UAS/bird cadence interpretation \cite{rohling1983,gandhi1988,kay1998,chen2011,tahmoush2015,rahman2018,molchanov2014}.
+
+The learned controls are not the main claim. Tabular and sequence ML baselines test whether ordinary supervised learners can exploit the detector-facing features under the same split. They are useful controls because they expose whether the schema is too easy, but the main comparator remains accepted human-engineered prior fusion. That choice matters: EI is compared against an already-fused human baseline rather than a single weak branch.
